@page "/aquaculture-oxygenation"
@inject LocalizationService Loc

<PageTitle>@Loc.T("aqox.pagetitle", "Aquaculture Oxygenation Systems | Oxyniti")</PageTitle>
<HeadContent>
    <meta name="description" content="@Loc.T("aqox.meta", "Nano-bubble aeration for aquaculture ponds, RAS and biofloc systems — raises dissolved oxygen through the water column, not just the surface, to prevent night-time DO crashes.")" />
</HeadContent>

<h1 class="section-title">@Loc.T("aqox.h1", "Aquaculture Oxygenation Systems")</h1>

<div class="content-section">
    <p>
        @Loc.T("aqox.intro", "Oxyniti builds nano-bubble aeration systems for aquaculture — engineered to keep dissolved oxygen (DO) high throughout the water column, not just at the surface, so farmed fish and shrimp stay healthy through the night.")
    </p>

    <h2>@Loc.T("aqox.why.h", "Why dissolved oxygen matters")</h2>
    <p>
        @Loc.T("aqox.why.p", "After sunset, algae stop producing oxygen and start consuming it. In a conventional pond, dissolved oxygen can fall sharply by the early hours of the morning — the danger zone for every farmed species. Fish under oxygen stress eat less, convert feed poorly, and a single severe crash can wipe out months of investment.")
    </p>

    <h2>@Loc.T("aqox.how.h", "How Oxyniti raises dissolved oxygen")</h2>
    <p>
        @Loc.T("aqox.how.p", "A conventional paddle-wheel aerator splashes oxygen in at the surface; large bubbles rise and burst within seconds, and most of that oxygen escapes back to the air. Oxyniti's nano-shear chamber instead draws pond water and injects air through a nano-shear process, generating bubbles under 200 nanometres across. At that size the bubbles are neutrally buoyant — they stay suspended in the water column and keep transferring oxygen for an extended period, rather than surfacing and escaping immediately.")
    </p>

    <h2>@Loc.T("aqox.where.h", "Where it applies")</h2>
    <p>
        @Loc.T("aqox.where.p", "The same oxygen-transfer approach applies across pond-based grow-out, hatcheries, and higher-density systems such as biofloc and recirculating aquaculture (RAS), wherever maintaining dissolved oxygen is a limiting factor on stocking density or growth.")
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="/#farming-types">@Loc.T("aqox.link.species", "Species we support: tilapia/GIFT, murrel, pangasius, freshwater prawn, carp polyculture")</a></li>
        <li><a href="/ras-oxygenation">@Loc.T("aqox.link.ras", "Oxygenation for RAS and biofloc systems")</a></li>
        <li><a href="/technology">@Loc.T("aqox.link.tech", "How the nano-bubble technology works")</a></li>
        <li><a href="/products">@Loc.T("aqox.link.products", "View products")</a></li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        @Loc.T("aqox.cta", "Talk to us about sizing a system for your pond or facility.")
        <a href="/contact">@Loc.T("aqox.cta.link", "Contact Oxyniti")</a>
    </p>
</div>

@code {
    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        Loc.OnChange += StateHasChanged;
    }
}
